# Connecting to the octo-pod pen — {{ p.id }}

{{ p.description | default('Third-party Zenoh peer.') }}

## 1. Your Files

```
tls/{{ p.cert_cn }}-cert.pem     your client certificate
tls/{{ p.cert_cn }}-key.pem      your private key — keep it private
tls/ca-roots.pem                 our CA, so you can verify us
```

Check they are intact:

```bash
openssl verify -CAfile tls/ca-roots.pem tls/{{ p.cert_cn }}-cert.pem
```

## 2. Open a Session in Client Mode

Keep your existing session to your own router. **Add** a second one pointed at us: a session
holds one TLS identity, so you cannot reuse the existing one or add our address to its
endpoints.

{% if _lan and not _host %}
**Ask us for the endpoint.** You reach us over the site LAN, from
{{ p.allow_sources }}, but this pod has no LAN address declared yet.
{% endif %}
```json5
{
  mode: "client",                                   // not "peer", not "router"

  connect: {
    endpoints: ["tls/{{ _host or '<ask us>' }}:{{ pen_listen_port }}"],
    exit_on_failure: { client: false },             // REQUIRED, default is true
    timeout_ms:      { client: -1 },                // REQUIRED, default is 0
    retry: { period_init_ms: 1000, period_max_ms: 4000, period_increase_factor: 2 }
  },

  scouting: { multicast: { enabled: false } },      // REQUIRED, default is true

  transport: { link: { tls: {
    root_ca_certificate:    "ca-roots.pem",
    connect_certificate:    "{{ p.cert_cn }}-cert.pem",
    connect_private_key:    "{{ p.cert_cn }}-key.pem",
    enable_mtls:            true,
    verify_name_on_connect: false
  } } }
}
```

The three REQUIRED lines override Zenoh defaults that break this:

- `exit_on_failure` and `timeout_ms`: a client that cannot reach us at startup exits instead
  of retrying. Our pod gets restarted; without these your session dies and stays dead.
- `scouting.multicast`: other Zenoh routers may share the network. With scouting on, you may
  attach to the wrong one.

`verify_name_on_connect: false` is needed because our certificate is issued for our mesh
identity, not the address you dial.

{% if _lan %}
You reach us over the site LAN, and only from {{ p.allow_sources }}. A connection from any
other address is dropped before TLS starts, which looks like a timeout on your side.
{% else %}
You reach us over the mesh. Ask us if you need to connect from the site LAN instead.
{% endif %}

## 3. Publish to `{{ _write }}/…` — Bare Keys

```
session.put("{{ _write }}/<anything you like>", payload)
```

Structure below that prefix is yours; we keep it verbatim.

**Do not prefix your keys with an org, tenant or anchor id.** Our router is default-deny:
keys outside `{{ p.namespace }}` are discarded silently, and your side still reports a
successful publish.

## 4. Subscribe

{% if _reads %}
You may read these keys, and only these:

```
{% for k in _reads %}
{{ k }}
{% endfor %}
```

```
session.declare_subscriber("{{ _reads[0] }}", on_sample)
```

They are released to you one key at a time; a subscription anywhere else receives nothing,
silently, the same way a denied publish looks successful. If you expect data on a key that
is listed here and see none, tell us: the release may be open while the source is quiet.
{% else %}
You have no read access. Your certificate may publish to `{{ p.namespace }}` and nothing
else. Ask us if you need to subscribe to something, and we will release it to you by key.
{% endif %}

## 5. Send Us

1. **Your Zenoh version.** We are pinned to {{ zenoh_image }}. Different releases may not
   interoperate; send this before anything else.
2. **Reachability:** `nc -vz {{ _host or '<our address>' }} {{ pen_listen_port }}`
3. **The key structure** you will publish under `{{ _write }}/`.
4. **Your payload format**, and a schema if you have one. Without one it is opaque bytes.

## 6. Wait for Our Confirmation

**A connected session does not mean it worked.** A rejected key looks identical to a
successful publish from your end: a denied put produces no log line on either side. We will
confirm the key, size and rate we actually receive. Until then, assume nothing arrived.

## If It Does Not Work

| Symptom | Cause, in order of likelihood |
|---|---|
| Connects, we see nothing | key prefix, see section 3 |
| Subscribed, you receive nothing | key not in the list in section 4, or the source is quiet |
| Session will not establish | Zenoh version, or `enable_mtls` and cert paths |
| TLS handshake fails | wrong CA file, or `verify_name_on_connect` not `false` |
| Cannot connect at all | reachability: `nc -vz {{ _host or '<our address>' }} {{ pen_listen_port }}`{{ ', and your source address must be ' ~ p.allow_sources if _lan }} |

Send us your client log. We see rejected keys and failed handshakes that you cannot.

---
Your data stays in our local pen with an audit trail.
